```sql
-- Enable RLS on our tables
ALTER TABLE screens ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE blocks ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

-- Policy for viewing screens
CREATE POLICY "View screens based on permissions" ON screens
FOR SELECT USING (
  (SELECT raw_app_meta_data->'permissions' ? 'read:all' FROM auth.users WHERE id = auth.uid())
  OR 
  (published = true)
  OR 
  (author = auth.uid() AND (SELECT raw_app_meta_data->'permissions' ? 'read:own' FROM auth.users WHERE id = auth.uid()))
);

-- Policy for editing screens
CREATE POLICY "Edit screens based on permissions" ON screens
FOR UPDATE USING (
  (SELECT raw_app_meta_data->'permissions' ? 'write:all' FROM auth.users WHERE id = auth.uid())
  OR 
  (author = auth.uid() AND (SELECT raw_app_meta_data->'permissions' ? 'write:own' FROM auth.users WHERE id = auth.uid()))
);

-- Similar policies for blocks
CREATE POLICY "View blocks based on permissions" ON blocks
FOR SELECT USING (
  EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM screens
    WHERE screens.id = blocks.screen_id
    AND (
      (SELECT raw_app_meta_data->'permissions' ? 'read:all' FROM auth.users WHERE id = auth.uid())
      OR 
      (screens.published = true)
      OR 
      (screens.author = auth.uid() AND (SELECT raw_app_meta_data->'permissions' ? 'read:own' FROM auth.users WHERE id = auth.uid()))
    )
  )
);

-- Policy for editing blocks
CREATE POLICY "Edit blocks based on permissions" ON blocks
FOR UPDATE USING (
  EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM screens
    WHERE screens.id = blocks.screen_id
    AND (
      (SELECT raw_app_meta_data->'permissions' ? 'write:all' FROM auth.users WHERE id = auth.uid())
      OR 
      (screens.author = auth.uid() AND (SELECT raw_app_meta_data->'permissions' ? 'write:own' FROM auth.users WHERE id = auth.uid()))
    )
  )
);
```

3. We can then use our claims management functions to set and manage these permissions:

```typescript
import { supabase } from '$lib/supabaseClient';

export async function setUserRole(userId: string, role: 'admin' | 'editor' | 'viewer') {
  let permissions: string[];
  switch (role) {
    case 'admin':
      permissions = ['read:all', 'write:all'];
      break;
    case 'editor':
      permissions = ['read:all', 'write:own'];
      break;
    case 'viewer':
      permissions = ['read:all'];
      break;
  }

  const { data, error } = await supabase.rpc('set_claim', {
    uid: userId,
    claim: 'role',
    value: role
  });
  if (error) throw error;

  const { data: permData, error: permError } = await supabase.rpc('set_claim', {
    uid: userId,
    claim: 'permissions',
    value: permissions
  });
  if (permError) throw permError;

  return { role, permissions };
}
```

4. In your application code, you can check permissions before performing actions:

```typescript
async function canEditscreen(screenId: string) {
  const { data: userData, error: userError } = await supabase.auth.getUser();
  if (userError) throw userError;

  const { data: claims, error: claimsError } = await supabase.rpc('get_my_claims');
  if (claimsError) throw claimsError;

  if (claims.permissions.includes('write:all')) return true;

  if (claims.permissions.includes('write:own')) {
    const { data: screen, error: screenError } = await supabase
      .from('screens')
      .select('author')
      .eq('id', screenId)
      .single();
    if (screenError) throw screenError;

    return screen.author === userData.user.id;
  }

  return false;
}
```

This approach offers several advantages:

1. Flexibility: You can easily add or modify permissions without changing the database schema.
2. Consistency: The same claims are used for both application-level and database-level access control.
3. Scalability: As your application grows, you can add more granular permissions without major changes to your security model.
4. Simplicity: All authorization logic is centralized in the claims, making it easier to manage and audit.

By using this claims-based approach for RLS, we're leveraging the full power of our RBAC system and maintaining a flexible, yet powerful authorization mechanism that works seamlessly with our block-based content structure and Supabase backend.

# Markdown

We can leverage this markdown/mdsvex file structure with Supabase to create a powerful, database-backed content management system. Here's how we can integrate this approach with Supabase:

Let's break this down into steps:

1. Supabase Database Structure:

First, let's enable our `blocks` table in Supabase to accommodate the markdown content:

```sql
CREATE TABLE blocks (
  id UUID DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4() PRIMARY KEY,
  page_id UUID NOT NULL,
  type TEXT NOT NULL,
  content JSONB NOT NULL,
  order INTEGER NOT NULL,
  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(),
  updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()
);

CREATE TABLE pages (
  id UUID DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4() PRIMARY KEY,
  slug TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,
  title TEXT NOT NULL,
  author UUID REFERENCES auth.users(id),
  published BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE,
  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(),
  updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()
);
```

2. Parsing and Storing Content:

Create a utility function to parse the markdown/mdsvex file and store it in Supabase:

```typescript
// src/lib/utils/contentManager.ts
import { supabase } from '$lib/supabaseClient';
import { parse } from '$lib/utils/mdsvexParser'; // You'll need to implement this

export async function storeContent(slug: string, content: string) {
  const { frontmatter, blocks } = parse(content);

  // Start a Supabase transaction
  const { data: page, error: pageError } = await supabase
    .from('pages')
    .upsert({ slug, title: frontmatter.title, author: frontmatter.author })
    .select()
    .single();

  if (pageError) throw pageError;

  // Store each block
  const { error: blocksError } = await supabase.from('blocks').upsert(
    blocks.map((block, index) => ({
      page_id: page.id,
      type: block.type,
      content: block.content,
      order: index
    }))
  );

  if (blocksError) throw blocksError;

  return page;
}
```

3. Retrieving Content:

Create a function to retrieve the content from Supabase:

```typescript
// src/lib/utils/contentManager.ts
export async function getContent(slug: string) {
  const { data: page, error: pageError } = await supabase
    .from('pages')
    .select('*, blocks(*)')
    .eq('slug', slug)
    .single();

  if (pageError) throw pageError;

  return {
    frontmatter: {
      title: page.title,
      author: page.author,
      date: page.created_at
    },
    blocks: page.blocks.sort((a, b) => a.order - b.order)
  };
}
```

4. Real-time Updates:

Implement real-time updates using Supabase's real-time features:

```astro
