6. Permissions and Row-Level Security:

Implement Row-Level Security (RLS) in Supabase to control access to content:

```sql
-- Only allow reading published pages or pages authored by the user
ALTER TABLE pages ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
CREATE POLICY "Public can view published pages" ON pages
  FOR SELECT USING (published = true);
CREATE POLICY "Users can view their own pages" ON pages
  FOR SELECT USING (auth.uid() = author);

-- Only allow editing by the author
CREATE POLICY "Users can update their own pages" ON pages
  FOR UPDATE USING (auth.uid() = author);

-- Similar policies for the blocks table
ALTER TABLE blocks ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
CREATE POLICY "Public can view blocks of published pages" ON blocks
  FOR SELECT USING (
    EXISTS (
      SELECT 1 FROM pages
      WHERE pages.id = blocks.page_id AND pages.published = true
    )
  );
CREATE POLICY "Users can view blocks of their own pages" ON blocks
  FOR SELECT USING (
    EXISTS (
      SELECT 1 FROM pages
      WHERE pages.id = blocks.page_id AND pages.author = auth.uid()
    )
  );
```

This setup allows you to:

1. Store markdown/mdsvex content in a structured way in Supabase.
2. Retrieve and display content efficiently.
3. Implement real-time updates for collaborative editing.
4. Control access to content using Supabase's authentication and RLS features.

The beauty of this approach is that it combines the simplicity of markdown authoring with the power of a database-backed system. Content creators can write in markdown, while your application can work with structured data, enabling features like:

- Advanced querying and filtering of content
- Real-time collaboration
- Granular access control
- Version history (by adding a versions table)

This system is extensible - you can easily add new block types or metadata fields as your needs evolve. It's also performant, as Supabase (built on PostgreSQL) can efficiently handle complex queries and real-time subscriptions.

